 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "The TatarTTS dataset comprises speech recordings from two speakers, one male and one female speaker. The statistics of the dataset is shown in Table I. In total, the dataset contains around 70 hours of audio consisting of over 38,000 segments."}, {"ref": 2, "quote": "The dataset comprises approximately 70 hours of transcribed audio recordings, featuring two professional speakers (one male and one female)."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "The TatarTTS dataset contains around 70 hours of speech [1], recorded by two professional speakers, one male and one female [2].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks how the apertium-anaphora module changed the accuracy of anaphor translation in its preliminary evaluation; passage 1 states that for Spanish–English translation, use of the module led to an increase in accuracy of anaphor translation, but for Catalan–Italian it resulted in a slight decrease in the accuracy of resolution.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "For Spanish–English translation, use of the module led to an increase in accuracy of anaphor translation, but for Catalan–Italian it resulted in a slight decrease in the accuracy of resolution."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "The apertium-anaphora module increased the accuracy of anaphor translation for Spanish–English by 25 percentage points (from 29.2% to 54.2%) but caused a slight decrease in accuracy for Catalan–Italian (from 83.3% to 75.0%) [1].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks why the apertium-recursive module was developed and how it works; passage 2 states both.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 2, "quote": "To deal with the limitations of finite-state chunking, the apertium-recursive module (Swanson et al. 2021) was developed by Daniel Swanson as part of Google Summer of Code 20193 to apply structural transfer rules recursively using contextfree grammars (CFGs) and a Generalized Left-right Right-reduce (GLR) parser."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "The apertium-recursive module was developed to deal with the limitations of finite-state chunking and to apply structural transfer rules recursively using context-free grammars (CFGs) and a Generalized Left-right Right-reduce (GLR) parser [2].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks for the three golden rules of fat loss; passage 1 and 4 state them exactly.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "1. Calories 2. Protein 3. Consistency"}, {"ref": 4, "quote": "When you boil it down, there are only three dietary “rules” you need to follow if you want to lose fat while retaining (or even gaining) muscle. RULE ONE​: Rule number one concerns the amount of energy - measured in calories - that you get from your diet. RULE TWO: Rule number two concerns the amount of protein you get from your diet. RULE THREE: Rule number three concerns the consistency with which you follow the first two rules."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "The three golden rules of fat loss in Gutless are: 1. Calories, 2. Protein, and 3. Consistency [1, 4].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks for the number of calories per day for a 185-pound person using the simple method; passage 1 states both the method and the calculation for this specific weight.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "For example, if you currently weigh 185 pounds, your estimated calorie intake for fat loss is somewhere between 1850 calories (185 x 10 = 1850) and 2220 calories (185 x 12 = 2220) per day."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "A 185-pound person should eat between 1850 and 2220 calories per day to lose fat using the simple method [1].", "clarification": ""}

