{"check": "Asks for the daily protein intake for a 220-pound person at 25% body fat; passage 3 states the calculation for lean bodyweight and protein intake.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 3, "quote": "If you weigh 200 pounds at 20% body fat, you have 40 pounds of fat (200 x 0.20 = 40). So your lean bodyweight is 160 pounds (200 – 40 = 160). A lean bodyweight of 160 pounds means you'd eat roughly 160 grams of protein per day."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "A 220-pound person at 25% body fat should eat approximately 180 grams of protein per day [3].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks for the daily calorie intake for fat loss using the simple method for someone weighing 80 kg. Passage 1 provides the simple method for calculating calories based on body weight in pounds, but the question is about kilograms. Therefore, the answer requires a conversion from kilograms to pounds.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "Just take your current body weight in pounds and multiply it by a number between 10 and 12. Many people will find that the number of calories they need to lose weight will fall somewhere between those two figures."}, {"ref": 1, "quote": "For example, if you currently weigh 185 pounds, your estimated calorie intake for fat loss is somewhere between 1850 calories (185 x 10 = 1850) and 2220 calories (185 x 12 = 2220) per day."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "To calculate your daily calorie intake for fat loss using the simple method, first convert your weight from kilograms to pounds (80 kg x 2.2 = 176 pounds). Then multiply your weight in pounds by a number between 10 and 12. Your estimated calorie intake for fat loss would be somewhere between 1760 calories (176 x 10 = 1760) and 2112 calories (176 x 12 = 2112) per day [1].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks how much protein 100 grams of Quorn provides compared with the same amount of beef mince; passage 1 states both values.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "One hundred grams of beef mince (15% fat), for example, contains around 20 grams of protein. The same amount of Quorn provides around 15 grams of protein."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "100 grams of Quorn provides around 15 grams of protein, while 100 grams of beef mince provides around 20 grams of protein [1].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks how Apertium uses weights to choose between conflicting structural transfer rules; passage 1 states that weights can be learned using an unsupervised maximum entropy approach to apply different rules in different overlapping lexical environments.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "These weights can be learned using an unsupervised maximum entropy approach (Bayatli et al. 2020)."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "Apertium uses an unsupervised maximum entropy approach to learn weights, which allows different rules to be applied in different overlapping lexical environments [1].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks why the timing of a water preload should depend on your age; passage 2 and 3 state that the timing depends on age due to differences in gastric emptying rates.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 2, "quote": "A 30-minute water preload doesn’t seem to work as well in younger compared to older individuals, which may be down to differences in the rate at which water leaves your stomach."}, {"ref": 3, "quote": "As you get older, gastric emptying - which refers to the rate at which food and fluid leaves your stomach - tends to slow down."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "Gutless says the timing of a water preload should depend on your age because younger individuals have a faster gastric emptying rate, while older individuals have a slower rate [2], which affects how effectively the water preload influences calorie intake [3].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks how the baseline TatarTTS models were built and trained; passage 1 and 4 state the training process and model creation.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 1, "quote": "we have created preliminary TTS models as a baseline for the Tatar language."}, {"ref": 4, "quote": "We trained separate TTS models for each speaker, creating single-speaker models. Each model was trained on A100 graphics processing units (GPUs) on an NVIDIA DGX server."}, {"ref": 3, "quote": "baseline end-to-end TTS models were built and evaluated using the subjective mean opinion score (MOS) measure."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "The baseline TatarTTS models were built as a baseline for the Tatar language [1], and they were trained separately for each speaker, creating single-speaker models. Each model was trained on A100 GPUs on an NVIDIA DGX server [4]. These models were also built and evaluated using the subjective mean opinion score (MOS) measure [3].", "clarification": ""}

{"check": "Asks about the role of grapes in Gutless, both in the explanation of energy density and in the author's diet; passages 2, 3 and 4 state both aspects.",
 "citations": [{"ref": 2, "quote": "If you wanted to eat 150 calories of raisins, you’d be able to eat approximately 50 grams. Yet the same number of calories would give you almost 220 grams of grapes — more than four times as much."}, {"ref": 3, "quote": "Frozen grapes have an energy density of just 0.7. This compares favorably to a tub of Ben & Jerry’s Peanut Butter Cup, which has an energy density of 3.4."}, {"ref": 3, "quote": "Occasionally this dessert is in the form of a Magnum Mini (vanilla ice cream dipped in milk chocolate), or whatever happens to take my fancy when I go out for dinner. But more often than not, it comes in the form of some frozen grapes."}, {"ref": 4, "quote": "Most fresh fruits and vegetables have a low energy density."}],
 "status": "answer", "answer": "Grapes play a role in explaining energy density as they have a low energy density, with 150 calories of raisins (which are dried grapes) being equivalent to 220 grams of grapes [2]. In the author's diet, frozen grapes are used as a dessert alternative with an energy density of 0.7, which is much lower than high-energy-dense foods like Ben & Jerry’s Peanut Butter Cup [3].", "clarification": ""}
